NNAAMMEE
     aauuddiittdd — audit log management daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     aauuddiittdd [--dd | --ll]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aauuddiittdd daemon responds to requests from the audit(8) utility and no‐
     tifications from the kernel.  It manages the resulting audit log files
     and specified log file locations.

     The options are as follows:

     --dd      Starts the daemon in debug mode — it will not daemonize.

     --ll      This option is for when aauuddiittdd is configured to start on-demand
             using launchd(8).

     Optionally, the audit review group "audit" may be created.  Non-privi‐
     leged users that are members of this group may read the audit trail log
     files.

NNOOTTEE
     To assure uninterrupted audit support, the aauuddiittdd daemon should not be
     started and stopped manually.  Instead, the audit(8) command should be
     used to inform the daemon to change state/configuration after altering
     the _a_u_d_i_t___c_o_n_t_r_o_l file.

     If aauuddiittdd is started on-demand by launchd(8) then auditing should only be
     started and stopped with audit(8).

     On Mac OS X, aauuddiittdd uses the asl(3) API for writing system log messages.
     Therefore, only the audit administrator and members of the audit review
     group will be able to read the system log entries.

FFIILLEESS
     /var/audit     Default directory for storing audit log files.

     /etc/security  The directory containing the auditing configuration files
                    audit_class(5), audit_control(5), audit_event(5), and
                    audit_warn(5).

CCOOMMPPAATTIIBBIILLIITTYY
     The historical --hh and --ss flags are now configured using audit_control(5)
     policy flags aahhlltt and ccnntt, and are no longer available as arguments to
     aauuddiittdd.

